<interrogationes unitas="scriba_documentum">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I open a text document from a volumen, or create it if it is not there?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_documentum_aperire scriba_documentum_creare volumen_plagulam_condere" via="lib/scriba_applicatio.c">Try
      scriba_documentum_aperire first; it returns NIHIL when the
      manifest (key spatium/documentum, or just documentum for an empty
      spatium) is missing, does not parse, or has a width or height of
      0 or less (and when the history cannot be opened). Only then call
      scriba_documentum_creare with the width, height and checkpoint
      interval (0 means LXIV; a width or height of 0 returns NIHIL). creare does not look for an
      existing document: it overwrites the manifest and starts a fresh
      history at cursor 0 while old acts stay in the volumen. It also
      ignores the result of the manifest write (volumen_plagulam_condere),
      so a failed write is not reported. lib/scriba_applicatio.c does
      exactly this aperire-then-creare.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I record an edit so it can be undone?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_documentum_committere scriba_documentum_committere_coniunctum scriba_documentum_tabula">Edit
      your own working TabulaCharacterum (same width and height), then
      call scriba_documentum_committere with it. The library diffs it
      against the projection, appends that act and returns its seq; 0
      means nothing changed or the act was refused. Afterwards the
      projection equals your sheet. The projection from
      scriba_documentum_tabula is read-only: never edit it in
      place. Use scriba_documentum_committere_coniunctum to join the act
      to the previous one, so a run of typed chunks undoes as one
      step.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does a mutatio act contain?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_mutatio_computare scriba_mutatio_applicare">The
      EFFECT of an edit, not the keys: after trimming trailing blank
      lines from both sheets, the common prefix and suffix are kept and
      the act says replace deletae lines at linea with the child linea
      elements (indentatio if set, textus if not all spaces). Because it
      is computed from before and after, it is exact for any pair of
      sheets no matter what vim did, and replay never runs vim.
      scriba_mutatio_computare returns an empty chorda when the sheets
      are equal or differ in size.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what counts as a blank line in a scriba sheet?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_mutatio_computare tabula_initiare" via="lib/scriba_documentum.worklog.md">Every
      cell a space AND indentation -1, which is what tabula_initiare and
      line insertion and deletion write. A NUL byte is content (vim can
      write one into the last column), and so is a line of spaces with
      indentation 0. Sheets built with
      tabula_ex_literis_cum_dimensionibus set indentation 0, so every
      line counts as content and every diff runs to the bottom (see the
      worklog).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I escape arbitrary bytes into a safe STML attribute value and back?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_effugere scriba_solvere" via="lib/nomenclator.c lib/membrum__prompta.c lib/scriba_actiones.c">scriba_effugere
      writes backslash escapes for backslash, NUL, tab, byte 1, double
      quote (as \q) and \xHH for every other byte below 0x20 or from
      0x7F up, so the result never holds a raw quote or control byte.
      scriba_solvere reverses it into a buffer you provide and returns
      the length, or -1 on a bad escape or when capacitas is too small.
      The hex digits must be lowercase: \x7F is rejected (scriba_effugere
      itself always writes lowercase, so its own output round-trips).
      Other code reuses this codec: lib/nomenclator.c,
      lib/membrum__prompta.c and lib/scriba_actiones.c.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a bad or oversized act damage the sheet?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_mutatio_applicare">No.
      scriba_mutatio_applicare decodes all new lines into scratch first
      and returns FALSUM with the sheet untouched if the root is not
      mutatio, a child is not linea, the range runs past the content,
      a line is wider than the sheet, an escape is bad, or the result
      would have more lines than the sheet height.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I undo, redo, and check that the projection is not corrupt?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_documentum_revocare scriba_documentum_reficere scriba_documentum_verificare scriba_documentum_cursor scriba_documentum_finis">scriba_documentum_revocare
      and scriba_documentum_reficere move the history cursor (FALSUM at
      the start or end) and rebuild the projection by replaying from the
      nearest live checkpoint; each successful move also appends a
      &lt;cursor ad=&quot;seq&quot;/&gt; note to the volumen, so aperire returns to
      that place (unless an act followed the note). A new commit after an undo appends a branch first,
      and the undone acts stay in the volumen as dead acts.
      scriba_documentum_verificare replays everything from nothing and
      compares seals. Compare scriba_documentum_cursor with the value
      you saw last to notice that another view committed, then copy the
      projection into your working sheet (lib/scriba_applicatio.c does
      this).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep one document open for a long session without the piscina growing?
    <responsum symbolum="scriba_documentum_committere scriba_mutatio_applicare scriba_documentum_revocare">Not
      really: every commit computes the diff and every applied act parses
      STML and allocates scratch in the piscina given at creation, and
      undo or redo replays acts from the last checkpoint the same way.
      None of it is released until that piscina is destroyed, so memory
      grows with the number of edits and undos.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
